Situation:
The sustainment brigade is directing ground distribution from multiple forward supply depots (e.g., Supply Depot Alpha and Supply Depot Bravo) to dispersed maneuver and support elements at forward operating bases and combat outposts (e.g., Infantry Battalion Falcon at FOB Archer, Armored Battalion Iron at COP Ridge, Artillery Battalion Thunder at Fire Base Orion). Priority commodities are ammunition and fuel. The main valley corridor (MSR Copper) is exposed to observation and harassing fires; movement requires deliberate security posture. The mountain approach (ASR Granite) is weather-restricted and capacity-limited by grade, with a chokepoint at a single-span bridge. Alternate lateral routes are longer with higher consumption and congestion. Airlift is constrained and reserved for emergency backhaul; ground convoys remain the primary mode.

Mission:
Deliver required supplies from forward depots to supported units over the planning horizon while minimizing total operational cost, to include transportation, controlled shortage penalties, vehicle deployment, and fixed route security risk costs, ensuring combat power is sustained without overextending the fleet or exposing convoys unnecessarily.

Execution/Concept:
- Distribution will be planned day-by-day from each depot to each supported unit by commodity. Outbound throughput from each depot is capped by daily on-hand and inbound receipts. Unit requirements must be met; controlled shortfalls are permitted but will incur a penalty and will be minimized.
- Vehicle loading is constrained by cargo capacity; commodity packing density drives how many vehicles are required per flow. Convoy sizing will respect capacity and road restrictions.
- A ground line of communication between a depot and a unit is considered inactive until we deliberately activate it for a given day. Activating any route incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing security overhead (escort teams, route clearance, ISR overwatch, QRF), regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route. Vehicles can only move on routes that have been activated.
- The total number of vehicles deployed across all routes each day cannot exceed the available fleet. Transport costs vary by depot–unit pair and reflect distance, terrain, and expected delays; risk costs vary by route exposure; shortage penalties reflect operational impact at the unit.
- The plan will consolidate loads on fewer activated routes where tactically acceptable to amortize fixed risk costs, while avoiding overconcentration on heavily threatened corridors. Pre-positioning will be used to buffer forecasted disruptions, and alternate routes will be activated only when their added risk and transport costs are justified by demand or to reduce penalties.
- Objective: minimize the sum of transportation costs, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs while meeting operational priorities and respecting supply, capacity, and security constraints.

